// ============================================================
// 4-Axis Cable-Driven Arm - arm_new_grip_updated_v4
//
// v4 CHANGE: yaw sensitivity is now ONE obvious constant, and it uses
// as much of the pot's travel as physically exists.
//
// The old map was a pair - YAW_POS_EXTREME_POT (600) with
// YAW_EXTREME_DEG (130) - which put 130 deg into the ~1460 counts
// between the boot zero (~2060) and 600. That's 0.089 deg/count, or
// ~24 motor steps per count. Replaced with:
//
//     YAW_POT_COUNTS_FOR_LIMIT = 2000
//
// meaning "the slave must move 2000 ADC counts from zero to reach
// YAW_LIMIT_DEG". 2000 is essentially the whole half-range available
// (zero sits near 2060 of 4095), so this is as much pot travel as the
// hardware can give: 0.065 deg/count, ~17 steps/count. That's a 1.46x
// reduction in sensitivity - real, but bounded by the pot, not by the
// code.
//
// IF THAT'S STILL TOO MUCH, the pot is out of room and the remaining
// options are mechanical or range-based, not tuning:
//   - drop YAW_LIMIT_DEG (90 instead of 130 buys another 1.44x)
//   - gear the slave yaw pot up so more hand motion = more pot travel
// The first is one number; the second is the real fix if you want fine
// yaw control across the full 130 deg.
//
// v3 CHANGE: yaw pot de-sensitized. Yaw gets its own hysteresis band
// and its own (slower) filter, because it's the touchiest axis by a
// wide margin - and that's arithmetic, not a bug:
//     shoulder:  90 deg over ~1360 counts * 177.77  = ~11.8 steps/count
//     yaw:      130 deg over ~1460 counts * 266.67  = ~23.7 steps/count
// Twice the motor motion per pot count as the shoulder. The 30:1 box
// makes the JOINT slow, but the pot still has to command 96,000 steps
// per revolution of it, so every count of pot noise is 24 steps.
//     YAW_POT_DEADBAND 12 -> 40 counts (~3.5 deg of slave motion
//       before the main arm follows)
//     YAW_FILTER_ALPHA 0.05 -> 0.025 (~80 ms time constant)
// The other three axes are untouched.
//
// v2: REWRITE of the teach/playback layer. Everything below the line
// "UNCHANGED FROM v31" is byte-for-byte the working code: pins,
// direction inversions, gains, bounds, compensation, pot conditioning,
// calibration, claw init, live-mode ramped stepping.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WORKFLOW
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//   boot      Slave arm aligned to main arm (straight up). Zero is
//             captured from the slave. Live tracking begins.
//   Y         (no points recorded) Set HOME = current pose.
//             (points recorded)    Smooth-move to POINT 1.
//   A         Capture a point: all four arm targets + claw angle, at
//             the instant you press it. Up to MAX_POINTS (50).
//             Live tracking continues between presses.
//   B         Stop capturing. Points are saved. You are "on" the last
//             point. Live tracking continues until you play something.
//   R2 hold   Play FORWARD, continuously: next point, next, ... wraps
//             last -> 1 and keeps going until released.
//   L2 hold   Play BACKWARD, continuously: previous, ... wraps 1 -> last.
//             Release either trigger -> arm parks at the point it's on.
//   D-pad R   Smooth-move to the HIGHEST recorded point.
//   D-pad DN + A   (only when on the highest point, and idle)
//             Resync to the slave arm and resume capturing. New A
//             presses append after the last point.
//   L1+R1+X   Wipe ALL points, resync to the slave arm, back to live.
//   D-pad UP  Escape hatch: abort whatever is happening, resync to the
//             slave arm, back to live. Points are kept.
//
// Every "smooth-move" and every playback hop uses the motion profile
// in the block right below this header. That's the only place speed
// and accel/decel live for playback.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW SIMULTANEOUS ARRIVAL WORKS (the "profile module")
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// One shared time profile p(t) runs 0 -> 1 over the move: a cosine-
// shaped acceleration lasting PLAY_ACCEL_TIME_S, a constant-speed
// cruise, and a cosine-shaped deceleration lasting PLAY_DECEL_TIME_S.
// Cosine shaping means velocity AND acceleration are both zero at each
// end, so there's no jerk at departure or arrival.
//
// Each motor's position at time t is simply
//     start + (end - start) * p(t)
// and the claw is
//     startDeg + (endDeg - startDeg) * p(t)
// Because every axis reads the SAME p(t), they all hit their endpoints
// at the same instant, by construction. The cruise length is chosen so
// that the axis with the biggest (distance / speed cap) ratio runs
// exactly at its cap - everyone else is proportionally slower.
//
// Where the elbow/wrist compensation fits: it happens in LIVE mode,
// exactly as it always has. A captured point stores the compensated
// SUMS (own + comp) in motor steps. Playback moves the four physical
// motors between stored sums. So compensation is baked into every
// endpoint, and the profile module never has to know about it.
//
// Recording tip: to grab something, capture "arrived, claw open" and
// then "same spot, claw closed" as two separate points. The claw moves
// on the same p(t) as the arm, so a single point with the claw closed
// would close it DURING the approach.
// ============================================================

#include <Bluepad32.h>
#include <ESP32Servo.h>
#include <math.h>

